nodes.prompt:
Brand: {{brand.brand_name}} — {{brand.brand_summary}}
Voice: {{brand.voice_tone}}
Value props: {{brand.value_props}}
Audience: {{brand.target_personas}}
Palette: {{brand.facts.palette}}

The usual way: {{inputs.theirs}}
What this brand does instead: {{inputs.ours}}
The verdict: {{inputs.verdict}}
Casting note: {{inputs.presenter}}

Cast ONE person from the target audience and write two spoken beats with a still comparison between them.

`actor` must pin identity down hard enough to repeat: apparent age, build, hair, face details, exact clothing including colour and fabric, and the specific room with named objects in it.

`master_frame_prompt` is a still-image prompt for the opening frame of this person in that room, looking into the lens. No camera brand names, no quality adjectives, no text.

`open_edit_prompt` is an EDIT instruction on the master frame for beat 1. Start with "Same person, same hair, same clothes, same room, still looking into the lens." then set a sceptical expression and a hand held out to one side.

`verdict_edit_prompt` is a second EDIT instruction for the final beat: the same person, settled and certain, hand on the other side.

`open_line` is spoken in beat 1: the usual way, at most 14 words.
`verdict_line` is spoken in the last beat: what they kept and the call to action, at most 16 words.

`compare_card_prompt` is a still-image prompt for an overhead flat-lay on a plain surface showing the two options side by side as physical objects — the usual way on the left, this brand's way on the right. Use the brand palette. Describe objects, surface and light only. No lettering, no labels, no text of any kind.

`on_screen_text`: exactly 3 captions, at most 5 words each — one per shot, and the middle one must name the difference.
